\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Flux in more than one plane}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{In dimension #{2m\ge4}, disjoint oriented coordinate planes can each carry a scalar field pulled back to the full grid. Their individual fluxes are defined on two-dimensional tori. The Wilson term adds the contributions of these planes, so the full massive kernel does not factor as the tensor product of their two-dimensional kernels. Does its half-signature depend only on the product of the plane fluxes in a sufficiently small scalar angle window? For a field that does not split into planes, the plaquette fluxes measure two-dimensional surfaces. An integer on a full #{2m}-cycle would require another construction.}
